WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.720
CHARLIE ENGELKE: Hello,
I'm Charlie Engelke,

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:10.030
and I work in Developer
Relations for Google Cloud

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:11.290
platform.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:14.642
My focus is on serverless
computing, and today's talk,

00:00:14.642 --> 00:00:16.059
we're going to see
how you can run

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:20.440
almost anything serverlessly
using Google Cloud platform.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:22.450
This is important
to me because I

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:25.940
was a software developer for
many years outside of Google.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:29.020
And I always wanted
to make the path

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.409
from it running on my machine
to it's live on the web

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:34.500
as short and simple as possible.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.250
And that's what
serverless computing does.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:39.520
It lets the developer's path be
as short as possible from when

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.650
your application is
working, as far as you

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.600
as a developer are
concerned, until it's

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.100
working as far as your
customers are concerned.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.480
Serverless computing
has a lifecycle

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.470
that, to really break it down
to its simplest possible terms,

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:56.440
is, the developer builds
the app, tests the app,

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.520
makes sure it's all working
in the local environment,

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.120
and then the platform takes
over and does everything else.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.030
It provisions computing
and storage resources.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.830
It handles logging, monitoring,
health checks, patching,

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:12.530
hardware problems, all that
stuff is the responsibility

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:13.700
platform.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.050
And as a developer,
in the past, that's

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:18.380
what I really wanted to do.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.290
I wanted to focus
on my application,

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.970
not on the platform.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:25.990
So serverless
computing is great.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:29.290
But it does have a deal you've
got to make as a developer.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.660
If you want to use a
serverless computing platform,

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.180
the platform offers
you this bargain.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:36.610
I'll provide you
specific runtimes--

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.960
that is to say,
environments for your app--

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.510
and if you want
to use me, you're

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:43.040
going to have to fit into
one of those runtimes.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.100
That's not terribly restrictive.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.430
Google Cloud platform has
several serverless platforms

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.665
with a lot of runtimes.

00:01:49.665 --> 00:01:51.040
For instance,
there's App Engine,

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.957
which has been around
for about a dozen years.

00:01:52.957 --> 00:01:55.150
And it supports half a
dozen different languages--

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:58.860
Python, Java, Node, PHP, Go.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.170
And Ruby is currently in beta.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.100
If instead of a web
application, you're

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.530
looking to deploy a web
service, Cloud Functions

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:07.060
is a platform for that.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:10.020
And it has for runtimes
it offers at the moment--

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.650
Python, Node, Go.

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:14.610
And Java is currently in beta.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.700
So if you've got an
application in any

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.970
of those very common popular
environments, you're set.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:22.790
You can use one of these
platforms as it is today.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.280
But what if you don't
have your application

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:26.858
fit into one of those runtimes?

00:02:26.858 --> 00:02:28.400
For example, what
if your application

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.700
needs to run a binary?

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.645
It's not just one
piece of source code.

00:02:31.645 --> 00:02:33.020
It's several
different components

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.070
that run as binary code.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.835
Maybe they're in
other languages.

00:02:36.835 --> 00:02:38.210
Maybe you've lost
the source code

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.700
or you've licensed
third-party code.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.770
Or they may incorporate
shell scripts.

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:44.930
There's a whole lot
of things that might

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.460
make you not fit into here.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.590
For example, you may
have started working

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:49.910
in a brand new language.

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.820
And it's a perfect fit for what
you need in your application.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.323
But you can't find a
platform that supports it.

00:02:55.323 --> 00:02:56.740
Well, in today's
talk, we're going

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.180
to talk about how to
solve that problem.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.020
But we're going to give
it a problem that's

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:01.620
the exact opposite.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.350
Instead of too new
a language, what

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:07.780
happens if you need to run a
45-year-old Pascal program?

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.360
To remind all of you may
not remember or may not

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.230
have been around, Pascal was the
most popular computing language

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.900
for teaching in
the '70s and '80s.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:20.680
And it was pretty well-defined
in this little slim book,

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.170
the "Pascal User
Manual and Report,"

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:25.180
written by Jensen and Wirth.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.510
It was published in 1974, which
is a full third of a century

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:31.840
before any of our modern
serverless platforms existed.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.060
So it's not terribly
surprising that the examples

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:36.820
weren't optimized to work
on serverless computing.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:38.770
But it does have a
program in it that we just

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.380
have to make available online.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.130
I found this program,
and I want everybody

00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:44.360
to be able to get the
benefit out of it.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.200
This is it-- the Pascal
program to convert numbers

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.170
to Roman numerals.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:51.750
It may seem fairly
simple, but this

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.540
has been around for 45 years.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.300
Any bugs in it presumably
have been well knocked out.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:01.450
You enter a number, and it gives
you back the Roman numeral.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:04.240
Now, in order to do this,
first thing I had to do

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.210
was look and see what serverless
platforms will run my Pascal

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:08.230
software as is?

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.720
And I could not find any.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:13.420
But I still want one,
because I don't want

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.810
to manage my own environment.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:16.282
I don't want to
run my own servers.

00:04:16.282 --> 00:04:17.740
I don't want to
package my software

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:19.323
and deliver it as
code that people run

00:04:19.323 --> 00:04:20.703
on their own infrastructure.

00:04:20.703 --> 00:04:22.120
I want to take my
application that

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.020
is working fine on
my local machine

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:28.440
make it available to anybody
that's got a web connection.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.840
Cloud Run lets me do that.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:33.420
Cloud Run is Google's
serverless platform

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.610
that offers a slightly
different bargain

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:36.690
than the traditional one.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.480
The traditional bargain is,
I'll give you a language

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.310
environment, you fit
into that environment

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.190
and you can run serverlessly.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:47.160
Cloud Run's bargain is, instead
of just bring your source code,

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.432
you have to bring a container.

00:04:49.432 --> 00:04:51.390
Now, when you first hear
that, you might think,

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:54.420
that is a lot of extra
complexity instead of just

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.670
bringing my own source code.

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.640
You've probably worked some
with Docker or seen about it

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.060
or used Kubernetes.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.510
And you know that containers
are extremely flexible,

00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:04.980
but there's a lot of
things you can do with it

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.230
and a lot of things you
need to learn how to manage.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.860
Cloud Run is actually a
very simple host environment

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:11.640
for a container.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:14.970
And it can be very easy using
tools on Google Cloud platform

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:16.962
in order to get this
out and running.

00:05:16.962 --> 00:05:18.420
And that's what
we're going to see,

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:21.360
the easiest way to take
our Pascal program,

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.080
package it in a container, and
let Cloud Run manage it for us.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.330
Containers can be a lot
easier than you might

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:30.023
think, easier than I thought.

00:05:30.023 --> 00:05:31.440
The first thing
is, you don't need

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:34.480
to install any of the
traditional container tools.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.360
You don't need to
install Docker,

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.480
because Cloud Build,
a Google service,

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.390
can actually take your
specification and your source

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:44.400
code and build the
container that you

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.798
need for you on Google's own
infrastructure, not yours.

00:05:47.798 --> 00:05:49.590
Not only that, you
don't have to figure out

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:52.057
how to build and specify
this container from scratch.

00:05:52.057 --> 00:05:53.640
There are a lot of
quickstart examples

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:55.073
that are close to what you need.

00:05:55.073 --> 00:05:56.490
And that's what
we're going to do.

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.130
We're going to take one of
those and squeeze in our Pascal

00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:01.260
program with minor
changes, changes

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.820
that don't require
container expertise

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:07.410
and don't require container
software on your own machine.

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:10.980
We're going to start with the
Cloud Run Python Quickstart.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.650
That's at cloud.google.com
/run/docs/quicks

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:14.820
tarts/build-and-deploy.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:22.890
If you go to that page,
you'll see that there are only

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:24.810
two files in this example.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.600
One is a very simple
Python web application

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.320
using the Flask framework that
just says, "hello, world."

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:33.030
Somebody asks for the home
page, it says, "hello, world."

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:34.360
That's it.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.210
The other part of
the quickstart is

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.820
a Dockerfile that is the
specification for how

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:40.710
to create the container,
which is to say,

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:43.905
the runtime that that
Python app requires.

00:06:43.905 --> 00:06:46.530
We don't have to change a lot to
make this work with our Pascal

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:48.010
code.

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:49.510
We're going to add
the Pascal source

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:51.040
code to the overall example.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.650
So instead of two files,
we're going to have three.

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:56.770
We are going to make the Docker
file compile it into a binary,

00:06:56.770 --> 00:07:00.650
because we can't launch the
Pascal program in source code.

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:02.358
We've got to convert
it to an executable.

00:07:02.358 --> 00:07:04.400
And then we're going to
tweak the Python program.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:06.200
So we're going to keep
the Python program.

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:08.117
But instead of it just
saying, "hello, world."

00:07:08.117 --> 00:07:10.955
that program is going to
invoke our Pascal executable.

00:07:10.955 --> 00:07:12.580
Let's see how that
all is going to work

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:15.590
and why it's going
to work that way.

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:18.850
The starting point, remember,
is that Pascal program,

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:21.860
written in 1974.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:24.810
Now, Cloud Run's bargain
is, give me a container,

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:28.180
but the container is supposed
to handle web requests.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:30.475
Cloud Run will take
incoming web requests,

00:07:30.475 --> 00:07:32.600
send them to your container,
wait for the response,

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:33.930
and send it back.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.020
That's really all it
expects your container to do

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:37.580
is handle web requests.

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:39.525
This code doesn't do that.

00:07:39.525 --> 00:07:41.150
That's because this
code was old enough

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:43.520
to drive before the
web was invented.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:45.590
This code was written in 1974.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:50.210
Tim Berners Lee told the world
about the web around 1990.

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:53.630
So not surprising
that 1974 software

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:55.340
doesn't handle web requests.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:58.400
Instead, it handles requests
from standard input,

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:02.430
and it sends the answers
out to standard output.

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:04.180
We're going to keep
it that way, and we're

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:07.220
going to let the rest of
our runtime environment

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:09.600
handle that for us.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:12.170
First of all, let's take
a look at the Dockerfile.

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:13.910
The Dockerfile in the
example is simple.

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:15.290
It's only six lines log.

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:17.700
And to deal with
our Pascal example,

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.620
we only need to add
three more lines to it.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:23.140
So the Dockerfile is not
terribly intimidating.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.540
Let's take a look at what
every one of these lines does

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:27.160
and why we need it.

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:30.063
The first line says what
runtime we start with.

00:08:30.063 --> 00:08:32.480
We're given a container that
can do a lot of what we need,

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:33.980
and we just need to add to it.

00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:36.730
In this case, the container
is a Python container

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:39.340
that can run Python programs.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:41.679
The last line of this
says, when Cloud Run

00:08:41.679 --> 00:08:44.530
invokes this, what software
inside the container

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:45.220
does it execute?

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:46.720
Well, what it's
going to do is, it's

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:50.290
going to execute the Gunicorn
web server using our Pascal

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:52.650
Flask program.

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:56.640
Now, in the middle, we have to
specify the working directory,

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.080
where all the software
is expected to be.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:00.810
We set that up to be /app.

00:09:00.810 --> 00:09:04.000
That is /app in the
container's file system,

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.000
not on our own local machine.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:09.390
And we copy all the software in
our current working directory

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:13.030
when we build the container into
the container at that location.

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:15.570
So that's how our Pascal
program gets into the container.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:18.650
That's also how the Python
program gets in there.

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:21.380
But again, the problem is,
the Pascal program's source

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:22.340
is in the container.

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:23.930
We need the executable.

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:28.100
So the next three lines
install software to do that.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:31.120
We're using the standard
Debian package installer.

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:34.080
And the first line is to update
it so that, when we install,

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.810
we get the latest versions.

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:40.240
Then we install FPC, which
is the Free Pascal Compiler.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:42.360
That's available,
and it will actually

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:44.110
do the job we need in
the next line, which

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:48.040
is to compile our roman.pas
source code into an executable.

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:50.800
By default, the output
of that compilation step

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:54.310
with the Free Pascal Compiler
will be a file called Roman.

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:56.330
Our working directory is /app.

00:09:56.330 --> 00:09:58.880
So we're now going to end
up inside the container with

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:03.450
/app/roman as a
binary executable.

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:04.980
All these run steps,
by the way, are

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.438
happening while the
container is being built.

00:10:07.438 --> 00:10:09.480
Once we have a container,
they'll all be finished

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.220
and they'll be ready to go.

00:10:11.220 --> 00:10:13.350
And then the final run
step we have in here

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:16.380
is installing the necessary
Python prerequisites.

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:19.410
We're running an application
that uses the Flask framework,

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.050
so we have to install Flask.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:23.040
And it's running under
the Gunicorn web server,

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:25.680
so we have to use a Python
package installer to install

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:26.970
that as well.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:28.800
When the container is
built, all the stuff

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:30.630
will be executed
and run and we'll

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:34.230
be ready to go ahead and have
the web server run our code.

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:36.710
Well, our code,
as we say here, is

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:39.980
going to be the Python app
launched under the Gunicorn web

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:40.480
server.

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:42.560
So let's take a look
at that Python app.

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:45.080
The starting point in the
quickstart is a simple, "hello,

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:45.940
world."

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:47.840
And the Flask
framework basically

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:51.890
says, here is a path that
might come in for a request,

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:54.480
and here's the code you
run to respond to it.

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.560
So we have here,
@app.route, slash.

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:59.900
The home page path--
just plain slash--

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:01.947
will run the "hello,
world" method.

00:11:01.947 --> 00:11:03.530
And all that method
does is, it checks

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:06.072
to see if there's an environment
variable that will say hello

00:11:06.072 --> 00:11:07.730
to that instead
of "hello, world."

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:10.297
By default, it then falls
back to using "hello, world."

00:11:10.297 --> 00:11:11.630
And then it returns that string.

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:12.590
Hello, world.

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:16.080
That's it, very
straightforward, very simple.

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:17.990
What we need to
do is modify this

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:21.548
so that it actually takes
a number and figures

00:11:21.548 --> 00:11:23.090
out the Roman numerals
for the number

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:24.315
and sends the result back.

00:11:24.315 --> 00:11:25.690
So first of all,
how are we going

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:27.080
to get the number in there?

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:29.810
Well, the easiest way I know of
is for somebody using their web

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:31.280
browser to put it in the URL.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:33.846
So instead of requesting
the web page at slash,

00:11:33.846 --> 00:11:36.980
request the page as
slash some number.

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:39.790
In Flask, you can specify
that as a placeholder,

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:42.370
and then say, when you run the
"hello, world" method, pass it

00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:44.720
that number, which
will be bytes.

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:46.450
We convert that to a string.

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:47.950
And now we have
something that we're

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:51.740
ready to ask the Pascal
program to parse and manage.

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:55.040
So the next step is to run the
Pascal program using the Linux

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:56.560
subprocess module.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:58.660
We tell it to run our
executable, which, again,

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:00.910
is at /app/roman.

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:03.520
We tell it, for standard
input, send it that string

00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:05.970
that we got, the
number to be converted,

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.080
and to capture the
standard output, which

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:09.712
will be our Roman numerals.

00:12:09.712 --> 00:12:11.170
And then the final
step is, instead

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:12.700
of returning "hello,
world," we're

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:14.710
going to return the
standard output from when

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:17.040
we ran the Pascal program.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:21.020
That's it, not a lot
of complicated changes.

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:22.570
Let's go ahead and
take a look at how

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:24.460
to do this with Cloud Run.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:26.865
We're going to have to start
by building the container,

00:12:26.865 --> 00:12:28.990
so let's go over to a demo
where we get it started.

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:30.490
Because that can
take a few minutes,

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:32.710
because installing that
Free Pascal Compiler

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:34.510
has a lot of prerequisites.

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:36.350
After we run this
command, we're going

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:38.890
to come back here, talk
about how the command worked,

00:12:38.890 --> 00:12:42.260
and what we need to do next
to actually get it to run.

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:44.420
We've created all the
pieces of our application,

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:46.930
and now we want to have it
running on the Cloud Run

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:48.112
platform.

00:12:48.112 --> 00:12:50.320
In order to do this, we've
got to create a container,

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:52.720
and we've got to then send
the container to the Cloud Run

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:53.740
service.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:55.510
That can all be done
from the command

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:57.140
line on your local machine.

00:12:57.140 --> 00:12:59.140
But I'm going to actually
do it without anything

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:00.220
installed locally.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:02.470
And in order to do this, I'm
going to the Google Cloud

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:05.020
Console at
console.cloud.google.com.

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:09.520
This site is where you can
create a new Google Cloud

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:10.690
platform project.

00:13:10.690 --> 00:13:12.190
And everything you
do is going to be

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:14.763
in the context of a project,
so we have to create one.

00:13:14.763 --> 00:13:16.930
And your project is going
to be a name you give it--

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:19.150
in my case, Engelke Next.

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:21.010
And it'll create a
system-wide project

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:24.280
ID, which, in many cases,
can be the exact same thing,

00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:27.940
if the name you picked
is globally unique.

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:30.300
I'm going to build it still
with the command-line tools.

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:32.300
And in order to do that,
instead of running them

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:35.160
on my local machine, I'm
going to activate Cloud Shell.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:38.760
Cloud Shell comes with
the Cloud Console.

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:42.480
Any web browser can let you
connect to it, any modern web

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:46.680
browser, whether you're
on Windows, Mac, Linux,

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:49.140
a chromebook, you name it.

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:50.700
Once you connect
to it, it's going

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:52.930
to give you a pre-provisioned
Linux instance,

00:13:52.930 --> 00:13:54.970
and you can run
the commands there.

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:57.430
Let's take a look at what's
already in my instance.

00:13:57.430 --> 00:13:59.370
I've been here before,
so I've already

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:02.867
got a directory with the source
code we've seen so far in it.

00:14:02.867 --> 00:14:04.950
There are a couple of ways
you can get that source

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:08.400
code onto your Linux
instance for Cloud Shell.

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:12.790
One is, you've got tools
like Git already installed,

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:15.000
so you can get something
from a Git Repo.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.960
Or you can use the commands over
here in a menu to upload files

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:20.430
from your local machine.

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:22.180
There are a variety
of ways to do it.

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:23.730
In this case, they're
already there.

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:27.400
So let's take a look at the
files that we've created.

00:14:27.400 --> 00:14:31.550
And go ahead and
build the container.

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:34.000
We want to build it in the
Google Cloud platform using

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:36.250
the Google Cloud Build service.

00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:40.630
The command for that is gcloud
builds, with an S, Submit.

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:42.730
And then we give it a tag
so we can find it later

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:44.140
in order to deploy it.

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:48.735
That tag is going to be in an
artifact repository in gcr.io.

00:14:48.735 --> 00:14:50.110
In this case, I've
named it based

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:51.930
on my project, Engelke Next.

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:53.650
And I've given it
a service name.

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:55.310
It could be pretty
much anything.

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:56.830
Since this is for
Roman numerals,

00:14:56.830 --> 00:14:59.170
I picked a service called Roman.

00:14:59.170 --> 00:15:02.350
If this is a first time you've
used the Cloud Build service,

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:05.440
you may be prompted that
it's got to be activated.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:07.080
And it'll offer to
do that for you.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:09.810
It'll add a couple of
minutes to do the build.

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:12.925
Normally, building a container
can be relatively quick.

00:15:12.925 --> 00:15:15.050
But in this case, it's
going to take quite a while.

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:17.590
And that's because we have
this container installing

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:20.560
software, including the
Free Pascal Compiler.

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.720
That turns out to have an
awful lot of dependencies

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:25.390
and it's going to take a
while to do that full build.

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:28.210
So we're going to go
ahead, go back to our talk

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:30.010
and see what the
commands actually

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:31.545
mean that we've just done.

00:15:31.545 --> 00:15:33.920
And we're going to see what
other commands we need to do.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:37.990
We'll come back here after
this build is finished.

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:42.880
All right, what we just did was
use the Cloud Shell in the web

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:45.430
console for Google
Cloud Platform

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:48.940
to actually invoke a command
that's going to run Cloud Build

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:50.260
and build our container.

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:52.343
Let's take a look at all
the pieces of the command

00:15:52.343 --> 00:15:54.100
and what we asked it to do.

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:56.885
The first part is the
gcloud command-line tool.

00:15:56.885 --> 00:15:58.760
We could run this locally
on our own machine.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:00.530
But if we did, we'd
have to install it.

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:02.110
We'd also have to
set up credentials

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:03.730
on our local machine
saying that we

00:16:03.730 --> 00:16:07.030
are authorized to actually build
a container using Cloud Build.

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:09.160
If we do this all
under the Cloud Shell,

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:11.440
everything is set up
for us ahead of time.

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:13.520
So we can run the
gcloud command,

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:15.930
but we want to run Cloud Build.

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:17.590
The subcommand for
that is builds--

00:16:17.590 --> 00:16:18.802
build, S.

00:16:18.802 --> 00:16:20.260
And what it's going
to do is create

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:22.018
a container using
the specification

00:16:22.018 --> 00:16:22.810
in the environment.

00:16:22.810 --> 00:16:24.850
We can specify it in a
lot of different ways.

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:27.040
By default, it's going
to look for a Dockerfile

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:29.680
in the current
directory and use that.

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:32.600
After it's done, we want it to
submit that container somewhere

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:35.860
so it's ready for us to
pass on to Cloud Run.

00:16:35.860 --> 00:16:38.440
We're going to submit it
to an artifact repository

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:43.120
and tag it with a name that is
based on our current project

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:43.950
ID.

00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:45.700
As you saw, the project
we were working on

00:16:45.700 --> 00:16:47.530
was called Engelke Next--

00:16:47.530 --> 00:16:50.320
I used my name to make
it [INAUDIBLE] unique--

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:52.100
and then slash, the
name of a service.

00:16:52.100 --> 00:16:54.580
And we can use just about
anything for that service name.

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:56.830
We used Roman because
this service is going

00:16:56.830 --> 00:16:59.800
to give you Roman numerals.

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:02.079
Once that's finished-- and
we'll go back in a minute

00:17:02.079 --> 00:17:04.329
and see that it's finished
and go on to the next step.

00:17:04.329 --> 00:17:06.121
Once it's finished,
we're ready to then ask

00:17:06.121 --> 00:17:09.040
Cloud Run to go ahead
and take that container

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:10.990
and deploy it serverlessly.

00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:13.750
And we use the same gcloud
command-line interface

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:14.599
to do it.

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:17.349
But in this case, since we're
talking to the Cloud Run

00:17:17.349 --> 00:17:19.630
service, the subcommand is run.

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:21.369
So it's going to
be a gcloud run.

00:17:21.369 --> 00:17:24.550
And then the action we want
it to perform to deploy it.

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:26.569
We need to tell it
what image to deploy.

00:17:26.569 --> 00:17:28.329
And it's in that same
artifact repository.

00:17:28.329 --> 00:17:30.370
We tell it how it was tagged.

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:32.920
And then we've got to tell
it where to deploy it.

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:36.490
Because Cloud Run is available
on Google Cloud platform fully

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:39.880
managed, but it's also
available on an environment

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:42.430
you manage, either on Google
Cloud or somewhere else,

00:17:42.430 --> 00:17:43.843
like your own infrastructure.

00:17:43.843 --> 00:17:45.760
We've got to tell it
which of those to deploy.

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:47.950
And as you saw from my
talking about how much I

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:50.560
like serverless, you know
I like to do the least

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:52.270
and have the platform
do the most, which

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:55.960
means I want to deploy it
to the managed platform.

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:58.120
And then the final thing
we have to put in here

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:00.190
is something about who's
allowed to run this.

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:02.950
Because by default,
Google Cloud Run

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:06.090
will require all web requests
coming in to be authenticated.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:09.310
It will only actually pass them
to your Cloud Run container

00:18:09.310 --> 00:18:11.950
if they're authenticated
by somebody you permit.

00:18:11.950 --> 00:18:14.260
Well, this particular
service, the Roman numerals,

00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:15.280
is too valuable.

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:16.840
We can't hold it
back from the world.

00:18:16.840 --> 00:18:18.382
We want everybody
to be able to do it

00:18:18.382 --> 00:18:19.880
without any authentication.

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:24.550
So we include the
allow-unauthenticated flag.

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:27.530
Let's go ahead back to the
demo, run this command.

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:29.685
It doesn't take quite
as long to deploy it

00:18:29.685 --> 00:18:31.810
as it took to build it
because of that install step

00:18:31.810 --> 00:18:33.160
we had in the build.

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:36.040
And then we'll see
how it actually works.

00:18:36.040 --> 00:18:37.850
The container is
finished building.

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:40.810
We can see that it was a
success and it was pushed.

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:44.230
So now we can go ahead and
deploy it with the gcloud run

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:45.610
deploy command.

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:48.760
Again, if it's the first time
you've done a Cloud Run deploy,

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:50.890
you may be asked to
activate some APIs.

00:18:50.890 --> 00:18:52.660
And it'll do that for you.

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:54.290
In this case, that's
already been done.

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:55.960
But it's going to ask what
I want to call the service.

00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:57.790
And it will infer
from the tag I gave it

00:18:57.790 --> 00:18:59.230
that I want to call it Roman.

00:18:59.230 --> 00:19:01.240
Roman Numerals,
that's a good name.

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:02.630
And then where to deploy it?

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:05.837
I'll go ahead and deploy it
to the US central 1 region.

00:19:05.837 --> 00:19:07.420
Now I'm going to
deploy the container.

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:09.545
And this takes a few minutes,
because it's not only

00:19:09.545 --> 00:19:11.090
going to deploy the service.

00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:13.030
It's got to set
up access control,

00:19:13.030 --> 00:19:15.190
even though we said,
allow unauthenticated.

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:16.810
And it's got to set
up traffic routing

00:19:16.810 --> 00:19:19.725
so that everything gets to
this cloud container properly.

00:19:19.725 --> 00:19:21.100
Once it's finished
running, we'll

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:24.310
be able to go take a
look at what URL is

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:26.210
and actually invoke it.

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:29.000
So while this is still
going ahead and running,

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:31.560
let's go ahead and, in the
top part of the console,

00:19:31.560 --> 00:19:33.550
go take a look at
the Cloud Run part.

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:42.550
And we can see this is being
built. It's not finished yet.

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:50.940
If we take a look
at the details,

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:54.150
we'll see it's
still in progress.

00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:57.240
Once it's finished, this
will show us the URL.

00:19:57.240 --> 00:19:59.252
Right now it says it's loading.

00:19:59.252 --> 00:20:00.960
And after it finishes
showing us the URL,

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:03.300
we'll be able to invoke
the service just by opening

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:05.700
a web page with that URL.

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:08.160
If we didn't tell it
allow-unauthenticated,

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:11.010
we'd have to send an
authenticated request, one

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:14.740
with a header that contains
proof of our identity.

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:17.350
This is going to be a
lot easier in this case.

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:19.800
All right, the revision
deployment is finished.

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:20.670
It's been deployed.

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:23.070
It's serving 100%
of the traffic.

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:24.720
And here's the URL.

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:26.410
When we invoke the
URL, we're going

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:29.000
to be disappointed
right at first.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:31.470
And that's because it comes
back with an error message.

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:34.310
Well, that error message
is not Cloud Run's fault.

00:20:34.310 --> 00:20:37.170
Our Python wrapper
program told how

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:40.980
to handle URLs that had
slash followed by a string

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:43.110
that we were going to
interpret as a number.

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:45.460
This URL does not
have that string,

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:47.410
so we didn't tell
it how to handle it,

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:49.500
so the Python
wrapper function just

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:51.780
doesn't know what to do with
it and returned an error.

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:53.850
Let's go ahead and
tell it, we want

00:20:53.850 --> 00:20:59.140
to take a look at the
Roman Numeral for 1974.

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:02.130
And it comes back
and gives us that.

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:05.588
It's not in the shortcut
for Roman Numerals.

00:21:05.588 --> 00:21:07.380
When they wrote this
program, it was easier

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:09.330
to write it this
way, and clearer

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:11.140
for the purpose of teaching.

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:14.250
But also the fact is,
in the earliest days,

00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:17.310
Romans didn't tend to use
the shortcut versions anyway.

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:18.547
Let's try another number.

00:21:18.547 --> 00:21:20.880
You may have noticed when you
were looking at my console

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:25.050
that I am doing this
recording on July 20.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:27.720
That is the anniversary
of the first lunar

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:30.510
landing on July 20, 1969.

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:33.810
So let's take a look at
1969 in Roman numerals.

00:21:33.810 --> 00:21:35.760
And again, it converts
it right away.

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:37.500
Anybody in the world
that knows this URL

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:39.000
can go ahead and
do this conversion,

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:40.710
as long as this is live.

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:42.900
The next slide, once
I show it in the demo,

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:44.570
will actually have
a URL that I'm

00:21:44.570 --> 00:21:46.440
going to be leaving
live for a few months

00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:47.898
if you want to try
it out yourself.

00:21:47.898 --> 00:21:49.520
Or of course, you
can deploy your own.

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:52.170
Now, this is not a very
easy URL to memorize or give

00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:53.220
to other people.

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:55.350
You can, if you have
your own domain,

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:58.620
actually configure Cloud Run
to respond with a page at one

00:21:58.620 --> 00:22:01.410
of the URLs on your own domain.

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:02.940
We're not going to
cover that here.

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:05.960
It's fully documented
in the web pages.

00:22:05.960 --> 00:22:08.430
OK, as you saw it was
pretty easy to actually

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:11.280
create the container without
having any local install

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:12.540
and to deploy it to Cloud Run.

00:22:12.540 --> 00:22:14.707
And now it's available on
the internet for everybody

00:22:14.707 --> 00:22:15.720
to access.

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:18.975
That URL that I did the demo
is for a throwaway project.

00:22:18.975 --> 00:22:20.850
It's not going to keep
running for very long.

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:23.405
But I still have this available
at this URL on the slides

00:22:23.405 --> 00:22:24.780
so you can come
back in any time,

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:27.000
and you go to that web
page, because you probably

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:30.445
need to convert numbers to
Roman numerals all the time.

00:22:30.445 --> 00:22:32.070
We're going to keep
this application up

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:35.043
for the next several months,
not promising it forever.

00:22:35.043 --> 00:22:36.960
Now again, this is not
the world's friendliest

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:38.520
URL to memorize.

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:41.700
It is possible to actually
use your own domain

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:44.603
and have your own URL pointing
to the service as well.

00:22:44.603 --> 00:22:46.020
We're not to cover
how to do that,

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:49.690
but it's fully documented
at the website.

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:51.610
We have now seen
that you can run

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:53.950
almost anything serverlessly.

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:55.840
And you do that by
creating a container.

00:22:55.840 --> 00:22:58.990
And containers can be easy
with Google Cloud platform,

00:22:58.990 --> 00:23:01.748
with Cloud Run and Cloud
Build to create them.

00:23:01.748 --> 00:23:03.790
You can do that without
any local Docker install.

00:23:03.790 --> 00:23:05.475
And if you use
Google Cloud Shell,

00:23:05.475 --> 00:23:07.600
you don't need to have
anything on your own machine

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:11.200
except a web browser, which,
if you're watching this talk,

00:23:11.200 --> 00:23:13.970
we know you've got a web
browser available to you.

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:15.940
You can start with a lot
of Dockerfile samples

00:23:15.940 --> 00:23:17.450
that are close to what you need.

00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:19.360
So you don't have to
learn how to create

00:23:19.360 --> 00:23:22.700
Dockerfile specifications
from scratch.

00:23:22.700 --> 00:23:26.150
And in fact, depending on what
you need your container to do,

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:28.840
you may not have to have
a Dockerfile at all.

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:33.010
Buildpacks, which are described
at buildpacks.io, that website,

00:23:33.010 --> 00:23:35.770
can eliminate the need
for a Dockerfile entirely.

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:38.470
They can actually look at
your source code structure

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:40.097
and create the
container from that.

00:23:40.097 --> 00:23:42.430
I don't think you're going
to find a buildpack, however,

00:23:42.430 --> 00:23:44.350
that can handle Pascal.

00:23:44.350 --> 00:23:47.328
But all said and
done, everything

00:23:47.328 --> 00:23:49.870
we did here is something you're
probably not going to do need

00:23:49.870 --> 00:23:51.285
to do exactly as given.

00:23:51.285 --> 00:23:52.660
You're probably
not going to need

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:56.027
to run Pascal software
on a serverless platform.

00:23:56.027 --> 00:23:58.360
But it's a lot more likely
you may need to run something

00:23:58.360 --> 00:24:02.650
in a language that's too new
to have much support on source

00:24:02.650 --> 00:24:05.170
code-oriented
serverless platforms.

00:24:05.170 --> 00:24:07.120
And in fact, it's
extremely likely

00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:09.880
that your application is going
to have many components that

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:12.370
include pieces you've already
got in binary-only form,

00:24:12.370 --> 00:24:14.530
either because they've
been around your company

00:24:14.530 --> 00:24:16.510
for a while, you've
lost the source code,

00:24:16.510 --> 00:24:21.790
or possibly, you've licensed
them in binary form only.

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:24.250
Or you may have other old
languages unlike Pascal.

00:24:24.250 --> 00:24:27.310
It's a lot likelier you may
need to run old Fortran or COBOL

00:24:27.310 --> 00:24:28.300
programs.

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:30.490
All of those are
things you can do now,

00:24:30.490 --> 00:24:32.300
because Cloud Run
greatly expands

00:24:32.300 --> 00:24:36.380
the universe of software that
you can run serverlessly.

00:24:36.380 --> 00:24:38.110
You can run almost anything.

00:24:38.110 --> 00:24:39.110
That's what we promised.

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:41.540
But there are a couple
of restrictions.

00:24:41.540 --> 00:24:43.460
We mentioned that
your software is only

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:46.402
going to be run in order
to process web requests.

00:24:46.402 --> 00:24:48.860
Cloud Run's not going to invoke
your software unless it has

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:50.583
a request for it to respond to.

00:24:50.583 --> 00:24:52.000
And after it gets
a response, it's

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:53.830
going to stop your
software from running.

00:24:53.830 --> 00:24:55.830
So you can't leave anything
running all the time

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:57.090
in the background.

00:24:57.090 --> 00:24:58.910
The other restriction
is, your software

00:24:58.910 --> 00:25:00.470
has got to be stateless.

00:25:00.470 --> 00:25:02.180
If you put something
in your source code

00:25:02.180 --> 00:25:03.888
that, for example,
has a global variable,

00:25:03.888 --> 00:25:05.270
keeping a count
of how many times

00:25:05.270 --> 00:25:07.723
you've received a
web request, well,

00:25:07.723 --> 00:25:09.140
every time a web
request comes in,

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:11.060
it may be going to a
different container

00:25:11.060 --> 00:25:13.172
and affecting a different
global variable.

00:25:13.172 --> 00:25:14.630
It's not going to
have any meaning.

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:17.450
If you need to keep state, don't
keep state in your container.

00:25:17.450 --> 00:25:19.430
Keep state external
to it, for instance,

00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:21.646
in an external database.

00:25:21.646 --> 00:25:24.150
So I hope this has
been useful, that you

00:25:24.150 --> 00:25:27.450
have seen that learning how
to run a 45-year-old Pascal

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:29.880
program on a modern
serverless platform

00:25:29.880 --> 00:25:31.890
can show you some other
techniques that actually

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:34.210
are more applicable
to your general use.

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:36.690
If you have any questions,
please use the Q&amp;A. We'll

00:25:36.690 --> 00:25:40.810
be answering them as quickly
as we can get to them.

00:25:40.810 --> 00:25:44.780
And again, thank you very
much for your attention.

